{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792590809,"nonce":"wVRuy+KDhYWSnMUjZH0wsPuexTY=","title":"Tiering: isolate lazy and promotion callbacks between live runtimes","message":"Two TieredRuntime instances in one process share a single process-wide lazy_compiler, lazy_optimizer, promotion_requester and worker-busy callback slot in compiler/osr.rs. Installing another runtime replaces the callback for earlier beads; TieredBackend::shutdown clears the slots unconditionally, including when that backend did not own the current callbacks.\n\nObserved while expanding TypeScript project-cache tests: a warm split-HIR host entry interpreted correctly but optimize_function(Baseline) intermittently failed with \"no baseline could be compiled\" when another in-process runtime fixture ran concurrently. The same fixture succeeds alone or when these runtime fixtures are serialized. The callback closure indexes beads belonging only to its own backend, so a request for another runtime cannot be routed correctly. This is separate from the fixed prelowered host-root adoption gap.\n\nRoute callbacks by runtime/bead ownership and unregister only the owner. Cover two live runtimes with lazy entries and real Cranelift/LLVM promotion; shut down either and continue calling/promoting the other. Preserve callbacks held by in-flight workers and bound registry growth after repeated runtimes. Do not use a process-global test lock as the production solution.","files":null}]}